# The Last Diagnostic

Tony Stark hadn't slept in thirty-one hours, and the coffee had stopped helping around hour twenty.

The workshop at the new Avengers compound hummed with the quiet static of machines that never really turned off — servers breathing cool air, a half-disassembled gauntlet blinking amber on the bench, three mugs gone cold beside a keyboard he'd stopped using an hour ago in favor of talking directly to the room.

"FRIDAY, run it again."

"I've run it eleven times, boss. The result hasn't changed."

"Then run it a twelfth. Maybe the universe gets bored of being right."

The problem was simple to describe and murder to solve: a signal, buried in cosmic background noise, pulsing in a pattern too clean to be natural. It had been bouncing off a relay satellite for six days, and nobody outside this room knew about it yet. Tony liked it that way. Panic was contagious, and so, annoyingly, was hope — he wanted to know what he was dealing with before he handed either one to the team.

The workshop door hissed open. Steve Rogers walked in holding two paper bags that smelled like actual food, not vending-machine food.

"You said you'd sleep after the briefing."

"I said I'd *consider* it. Considering is basically done. I considered it very hard for about four seconds around 3 a.m."

Steve set the bags down without comment — he'd learned, over the years, which battles were worth having with Tony Stark and which ones just wasted daylight — and looked at the holographic waveform spinning slowly over the workbench. "That the thing from the satellite?"

"That's the thing from the satellite." Tony flicked two fingers and the waveform expanded, resolving into a lattice of interlocking pulses, gold light scaffolding the air between them. "It's not random. It's not one of ours. And forty minutes ago it started repeating on a twelve-second loop, which either means it's a machine talking to itself, or—"

"Or it's a machine waiting for an answer."

"Yeah." Tony rubbed his eyes with the heel of his hand. "That's the one that's been keeping me up."

Steve pulled a stool over, sat, and for a while didn't say anything, just watched the pattern breathe gold across the room. It was a thing Tony appreciated about him and rarely admitted out loud — Steve didn't fill silences just to prove he was listening.

"You going to answer it?" Steve finally asked.

"I don't know yet if 'hello' is the right opening line for something that might not know what hello means." Tony leaned back, chair creaking. "This is either first contact or the world's most elaborate practical joke, and I would like to not find out live, in front of the cameras, mid-panic."

"So we don't tell the others yet."

"We tell them the second I know what 'it' is. Not before. I'm not sending Wanda and Sam into a room to stare at a mystery signal and speculate — that's how you get six theories and zero facts by lunch."

Steve almost smiled. "You've gotten more careful."

"I've gotten more tired. Careful is just tired with better PR." Tony reached for the nearest cold coffee, grimaced, drank it anyway. "FRIDAY, cross-reference the pulse intervals against every deep-space probe we've lost contact with in the last decade. If something out there is trying to phone home, I want to know if home is here."

"Running now, boss. Estimated fourteen minutes."

"Fourteen minutes." Tony glanced at Steve. "Enough time to eat something that isn't cold coffee, I guess."

"That was the idea," Steve said, sliding one of the paper bags across the bench.

Outside, the sun was coming up over the compound, gold light climbing the windows the same color as the pattern still turning slowly above the workbench — patient, waiting, twelve seconds at a time, for whatever answer Tony Stark decided to give it.
